# Cloudflare Pages and Netlify both read a `_headers` file at the site root with this exact syntax: a path pattern
# line followed by indented `Header: value` lines, `*` as a greedy wildcard (usable mid-segment, e.g. `*.wasm`,
# per Netlify's documented `/templates/*.html` example — Cloudflare Pages' docs describe the same splat mechanism
# and state that headers from every matching rule apply, later values for the same header appending rather than
# replacing). See https://docs.netlify.com/manage/routing/headers/ and
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/.
#
# Cross-origin isolation (COOP/COEP/CORP) mirrors main.ts's dev server (createHandler in main.ts) exactly: it is
# required for SharedArrayBuffer, which the threaded Wasm core needs (src/core/wasm/loader.ts::planCore). Every
# resource the app loads is same-origin, so these headers cost nothing; without them the app falls back to the
# single-thread core.
/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

# index.html and the (non-production) test harness must always be revalidated so a new deploy is visible on the
# next load, never served stale from a shared or browser cache.
/*.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# dist/assets/*.{js,mjs,wasm} are emitted with fixed filenames (scripts/build.ts does not content-hash them), so a
# redeploy can change their bytes without changing the URL. They can still be cached, but never as `immutable` —
# always revalidate before reuse.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

# Belt-and-braces content type for the Wasm core builds: some hosts' built-in MIME inference falls back to
# application/octet-stream for .wasm, which WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming() refuses to compile from directly.
/assets/*.wasm
  Content-Type: application/wasm
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate
